{"name":"Neon Boneyard","hex":"#dfc5fe","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"neon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol Ne) with an atomic number of 10. The second of the noble gases, it is a colourless, odorless inert gas.\n A form or sample of the element.","A form or sample of the element."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That resembles a neon light; extremely bright; fluorescent"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/neon"},{"term":"boneyard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A graveyard.","In the game of dominoes, the pile of upside-down pieces that have yet to be used."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boneyard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Neon Museum","blocks":[{"text":"The Neon Museum in Las Vegas, Nevada, United States, features signs from old casinos and other businesses displayed outdoors on 2.27 acres (0.92 ha). Efforts to establish a neon sign museum were underway in the late 1980s, but stalled due to a lack of resources. On September 18, 1996, the Las Vegas City Council voted to fund such a project, to be known as The Neon Museum. The organization started out by re-installing old signage in downtown Las Vegas, to attract more visitors to the area."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/TheNeonMuseumLasVegas1_%28cropped%29.png/330px-TheNeonMuseumLasVegas1_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neon_Museum","wikidata":"Q6993430"},{"title":"Neon","blocks":[{"text":"Neon is a chemical element; it has the symbol Ne and the atomic number 10. It is the second noble gas in the periodic table. Neon is a colorless, odorless, inert monatomic gas under standard conditions, with approximately two-thirds the density of air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Neon_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Neon_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source